Run a local server and use two browser sessions to try room chat, direct
messages, and sharing a session with another device.

# Start Rooms

Rooms runs in [your browser]($link.siteAsset('rooms/app/index.html')). For
this walkthrough, run a Rooms server on your machine to host the rooms you
and another person will join.

Install the [CLI + Rooms product](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/host.md#install)
and start the server. The installer verifies the release checksum, and the
product includes the server and its own copy of the page, so it needs no
compiler. To install by hand, download `glowcap-vVERSION-ARCH-OS.tar.gz` from
[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/releases).

```sh
curl -fsSL https://glowcap.org/install.sh | sh
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
glowcap apps rooms serve
```

Open the link printed under **open the page**. It includes your server's
address. The page asks once whether to use that server,
and your browser may ask whether this site may reach a server on your
computer. Say yes to both. The page loads from here, your session stays in
your browser, and the server on your machine hosts the directory that lists
rooms and hands out nicknames.

Peers reach the directory over encrypted [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')).
The server listens on loopback by default. See the
[deployment guide](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/deploy/README.md)
for Docker and public hosting, or [developing Rooms](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/developing.md)
to build and run from a checkout.

# Choose where your session lives

The first time you open Rooms, it asks where to keep your session: the
rooms you join, your direct conversations, your drafts, and your history
settings. Choose **Use this browser** to start.

[The first-run choice. Keep your session in this browser, or open one that another device or a server is already keeping.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-dark.webp'))

[The first-run choice. Keep your session in this browser, or open one that another device or a server is already keeping.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-light.webp'))

**Use this browser** keeps the session on this device. **Shared session**
opens one already running in another browser or on a server. Both offer the
same Rooms features. You can switch later in Settings.

# Join a room

The join card already points at your server. Type a room name such as
`workshop`. If no room has that name, joining creates
it. You can leave **Display name** empty: the grey suggestion is a name the
server can give you, and joining with the field empty takes it.

[The join card, pointed at your server, with a room name typed. The display name can stay empty.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-join-dark.webp'))

[The join card, pointed at your server, with a room name typed. The display name can stay empty.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-join-light.webp'))

If the name you type is taken, the card shows the next free one and will not
send a join it knows would fail. Rooms never quietly changes a name you typed.

Now open the same address as somebody else (a private window is the quickest
way), choose **Use this browser** there too, and join `workshop` under another
name. Each session is its own peer, with its own seat in the room and its own
history. A bot can join too. The
[CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) joins a room from a terminal as
`glowcap apps rooms session`, as shown on the [Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) page.

# Talk in the room

Type into the box at the bottom and press Enter. Shift+Enter starts a new
line. You can attach files and use simple formatting, and the command menu
lists what you can do in this conversation.

A line you send shows as pending until the room confirms it. If the room
refused it before anything happened, Rooms puts your text back in the box.
If nobody can say whether it went through, Rooms tells you that instead of
inviting you to send it twice.

Hover over one of your own lines to edit or delete it. Posting gave you the
reference that permits those changes.

# Open a direct conversation

In the member list, press the message button beside the other member. The
server introduces you, and the new conversation appears under
**Direct messages**. From here on, your messages go between the two of you, and
the room sees none of them.

[A direct conversation, opened from the member list. The header says what this connection is, and the card above the first message says who can read it.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-direct-dark.webp'))

[A direct conversation, opened from the member list. The header says what this connection is, and the card above the first message says who can read it.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-direct-light.webp'))

The default route is **Fastest**. On a page with a Rooms server it tries
WebRTC and the server at once and keeps whichever opens first. The server
often opens first, and then the conversation card names the Rooms server as
the path. Both are encrypted end to end. WebRTC goes straight between you
when it can find a route, and uses STUN and TURN servers when the network
needs them. On a page with no server, browser to browser is the only route.

To keep the server out of the path whenever WebRTC works, or to go through
the server alone, open Settings, go to the **Connection** tab, and under
**Direct message connection** pick **Browser to browser** or **Through the
server**. That setting only changes how direct messages reach you, not how
your session is shared.

[WebRTC signaling]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) explains offers,
candidates, STUN, and TURN. [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations'))
shows where a host supplies TURN credentials and the relay.

# Open your session somewhere else

For another view on this machine, open a separate browser or private window.
For a phone or another computer, first give the page an HTTPS address both
devices can reach, using the
[deployment guide](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/apps/rooms/web/deploy/README.md).
The loopback address printed at startup works only on the machine running
Rooms. Plain HTTP at a LAN address will not work because browsers withhold
APIs Rooms needs there.

Back in the first session, open Settings and go to the **Session** tab.
**Share this session** publishes it and shows you its link. Open the page
in the other browser, choose **Shared session**
on the first screen, paste the link, and press **Attach**.

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-dark.webp'))

[The same session opened on a phone. The room, its members, and the direct conversation all came with it, and the divider marks where the shared history ends and live messages begin.]($image.siteAsset('rooms-session-attached-light.webp'))

The phone shows the same rooms, direct conversations, and history settings.
A message sent from either device appears on both.
Closing or reloading the page that hosts the session disconnects attached
devices. The saved sharing links remain.
For a session that is always on, run the session service from
[Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')) and open it the same way.

# Keep and share history

Rooms can keep room and direct-message history in your session. A host can
also keep room history on the server, run an archive service, or hand
someone a read-only link to an archive. These stay separate, and you choose
which one new messages go to.

Files stay with their messages. A history entry can describe an attachment
even when the archive did not save its file. The
[operations guide]($link.page('rooms/operations')) covers history on the
server, its limits, and what a crash can lose.

# Other ways to open the page

You can [open the page]($link.siteAsset('rooms/app/index.html')) without the
printed link. The session setup screen names the Rooms server it will use.
If no server is selected, press **Choose…**, paste the **web ui** address your
server printed, press **Test**, then **Save**. The same choice is in Settings,
under **Connection**, and this browser keeps it.

If the directory's address includes `noise-relay`, its encrypted relay lets
your browser connect without a Rooms server.

The **web ui** address is also that same page, served by your server itself,
for a machine that cannot reach this site. A browser keeps a session per
origin, so the page there has a session of its own, apart from the one you
open here.

# Continue

[How Rooms works]($link.page('rooms/architecture')) explains what a click
promises and how the code is divided. To host Rooms or put it behind HTTPS,
continue with [Operate Rooms]($link.page('rooms/operations')). To connect an
IRC network or client, use [IRC and Rooms]($link.page('rooms/irc')).
